  <h2 class="subhead" id="algorithim">Algorithim</h1>

    This algorithim needs to allow for upsampling and downsampling of the input image on both the horrizontal and vertical axis. This will also
    ideally require no change in logic to switch between these modes. <br> <br>
    
    With this algo, the coordinate system from the source image will be translated to the destination image. Then the destination pixels 
    will be computed as the average of overlapping pixels in the source domain. This Algo is formalized below. <br> <br>
    
    If this project was to be done again, this module can likely be simplified alot without sacrificing much functionality. 
    This could include only allowing down-sampling, or making the output image classification size 32x32 rather than 28x28 to
    simplify some computations. <br> <br>

    <br> 
    The input/output of this module are essentially the two below arrays:

<pre><code class="language-bash">input source_frame[w-1:0][h-1:0] = {0xII}           // Intensity value 0-255
output destination_frame[28-1:0][28-1:0] = {0xII}   // Intensity value 0-255
</code></pre>


    The source frame can be shown instead as the below continous function: <br>
      $$ f(x, y) = source\_continous(x, y) = source\_frame[floor(x)][floor(y)],  {0 <= x < w, 0 <= y < h}$$

    Coordnates can be translated from the desitationt to source domain with the below equation:
      <!-- $$ x_{src} = w*i/28 $$ -->
      <!-- $$ y_{src} = h*j/28 $$ -->
      $$ \vec{R_{src}}(i, j) = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} i \\ j \end{bmatrix} * \begin{bmatrix} w/28 & h/28 \end{bmatrix}$$

    Where w, h are the width/height of the source pixel array respectivley. And 28 is constant as the output frame is always 28x28<br> <br>

    So the precise equation used for resizing is the below:
      $$ dest\_array[i][j] = \frac{\int_{A} f(x, y) dxdy}{\int_{A} dxdy}$$

    Where A is the area in between the points (i, j) -> (i + 1, j + 1) translated into the source domain. i.e. Area between two points below :
      $$ A(i, j):  \vec{R_{src}}(i, j) - to - \vec{R_{src}}(i + 1, j + 1) $$

    Given that the image may be upsampled or downsampled, the area of A may be less than 1 (upsampling) or greater than 1 (downsampling)


    <h2 class="subhead">Box Cofficent Update</h1>
      This logic is used to generate static cofficents in the resizing pipeline. 

      These coefficents will need to be updated when the input box coordanates change.

      Update time will be much less than 1 frame. <br> <br>

      These coefficents are calculated this way to reduce computations needed to be done in the pipeline and
      avoid having to divide, which is a many cylce operation.

      <p><img src="/imgs/resizing-coefficent-update-diagram.png" alt="img" width="50%" height="auto"></p>

      <pre><code class="language-bash">
topl[31:0] = { top_left_y[15:0], top_left_x[15:0] }     // coordanates of top-left corner to crop = (y1, x1)
botr[31:0] = { bot_right_y[15:0], bot_right_x[15:0] }   // coordanates of bottom-right corner to crop = (y2, x2)

width[23:0] = x2 - x1 + 1           // Width of the croped image. Fixed point 16b.8b value
height[23:0] = y2 - y1 + 1          // Height of the croped image. Fixed point 16b.8b value

dx[23:0] = width/28                 // How many source pixels map to one destination pixel. 16b.8b value. Used in horrizontal average filter.
                                    // Maximum value = 1280p/28 ~= 45.714, Minimum value = 1/28 ~= 0.03571
dy[23:0] = height/28                // How many source pixels map to one destination pixel. 16b.8b value. Used in vertical average filter.
                                    // Maximum value = 720p/28 ~= 25.714, Minimum value = 1/28 ~= 0.03571

factor_w = 28/width                 // How many destination pixels map to one source pixel. 16b.8b value. Used in horrizontal normalization
factor_h = 28/width                 // How many destination pixels map to one source pixel. 16b.8b value. Used in vertical normalization
      </code></pre>
      
      The coefficent FSM will capture input coefficents, and sequence the setup + capture of these values thorugh the DSP/Divider hardaware. 
      It will also synchornize the updating of the coefficents with the end of the current frame, so they will take effect at the start of the next one.


  <h2 class="subhead">Core 7-Stage pipeline</h1>

    <img src="/imgs/pipeline.png" alt="img" width="50%" height="auto"></p>
    
    <!----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->
    <h3>1. Sub-image filter (Crop) </h1>
      Only pass pixels which lie in the bounds of the given box.
      <p style="color: red;">
      >> Latency impact: 0 // non used pixels are just skipped. <br>
      >> Area impact: ~37 registers, minimal gates // State and counter logic <br>
      </p>

    <!----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->
    <h3>2. Luminance filter </h1>
      Convert the RGB pixel value into brigtness value: 0.299*R + 0.587*G + 0.114*B
      <p style="color: red;">
      >> Latency impact: 3 pclk cycles // 3 cycles given for 8-bit mult + add operation<br>
      >> Area impact: ~50 registers from 3 gate implmented DSP units <br>
      </p>

  <!----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->
  <h3> FIFO stage </h1>
    Adding timing allowance for next stage. 64 Pixels/Bytes deep.
    <p style="color: red;">
    >> Latency impact: ~3 pclk cycles<br>
    >> Area impact: 64 deep x 8-bits/pixel <br>
    </p>

  <!----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->
  <h3>3. Horrizontal Average Filter </h1>
    Sum pixels in the horrizontal direction. Output 1 pixel for every "dx" input pixels. 
    "dx" is a fixed point value that may be less than or greater than 1. So the sum may start + end at a fractional pixel value. <br> <br>

    So for each input pixel: sum += pixel_value * overlap, where overlap is  between 0 and 1. <br> <br>

    Pixel output is a 16b.8b bit fixed point sum. <br> <br>
    Max value is when dx = 1280p/28 * 255 ~= 11660 (down sampling 1280 pixel row into 28 pixel row) <br> <br>
    Precision was choosen based on worst case scenarios like when dx = 1/28, and a pixel has a intensity of 0x1. in which case 
    the output pixel here would be 0.035614, which can be captured as 0x9 in floating point format.

    <p style="color: red;">
      >> Latency impact: From last pixel input, 2 pclk cycles. Worst case limitation due to througput per horrizontal pizel row = ~ 64 pclk cycles<br>
      >> Area impact: ~286 registers <br>
    </p>

  <!----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->
  <h3>4. Normalizer 1</h1>
    Normalize the pixels. All pixels should be normalized by factor of "factor_w"=1/"dx". <br>
    So if you added up "dx" pixels, you multiply by 1/"dx" to get the average.
    <p style="color: red;">
      >> Latency impact: ~4 pclk cycles<br>
      >> Area impact: ~ 1 DSP unit <br>
    </p>

  <!----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->
  <h3> FIFO stage </h1>
    Adding timing allowance for next stage. 32 Pixels/Bytes deep
    <p style="color: red;">
      >> Latency impact: Worst case ~32<br>
      >> Area impact:  64 deep x 8-bits/pixel  <br>
    </p>
  
  <!----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->
  <h3>5. Vertical Average Filter </h1>
    Sum pixels in the vertical direction. Output 1 pixel row for every "dy" input pixel rows. <br>
    28 counters are maintained at the same time, Once a row is finished, all 28 pixels are output sequencially
    and the next row is started. Fractional start/stop positioning is maintainted.    <br> <br>
    This is split into 3 main sub-modules:
    <ul>
      <li>FSM for reading input pixels and tracking source + destination pixel coordanates.</li>
      <li>Pixel buffer for 28 counters, along with a Multiply+Add DSP unit which all counters share.</li>
      <li>FSM for sending out finished counts.</li>
    </ul>
    Otherwise operation is similar to horrizontal average filter. <br> <br>

    For each pixel: sum += pixel_value * overlap, where overlap is  between 0 and 1. <br> <br>
    Pixel outputs are int 16b.8b unsigned fixed-point format.  <br> <br>
    vsync / valid signals are maintained. 

    <p style="color: red;">
      >> Latency impact: Worst case ~10 pclk cycles from when last pixel on a row is input, to when first of 28 pixels is output. 28 pclk cycles to output a row.<br>
      >> Area impact: ~1300 registers + 1 MAC DSP. Including from 1 32x8-bit SRAM buffer for pixels. 1 32x24-bit for counters.<br>
    </p>

    <!------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------>
  <h3>6. Normalizer 2</h1>
    Normalize the the sums done in vertical average filter. All pixels should be normalized by factor of "factor_h"=1/"dy". <br>
    Output of this block is a 8-bit pixel value, ready for use in KNN classification.
    <p style="color: red;">
      >> Latency impact: ~4 pclk cycles<br>
      >> Area impact: ~ 1 DSP unit <br>
    </p>

  <h3>7. Stream to MM</h1>
    Write the 28x28 pixel stream in a SRAM buffer. Then signal to K-Means block to start the classification.

